#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662822 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,3,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨0,-1,k⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨2,5,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & k \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & k \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & k \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1\\cdot 1-5\\cdot k)-3(0\\cdot 1-k\\cdot 2)+2(0\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-1-5k)+6k+4 \\\\  & =k+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ k+3 & =0 \\\\ k & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662860 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,2,2),$ $B(4,2,1),$ $C(1,4,4),$ and $D(4,3,1).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+2j+k)-(i+2j+2k) \\\\  & =3i+0j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+4j+4k)-(i+2j+2k) \\\\  & =0i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(4i+3j+k)-(i+2j+2k) \\\\  & =3i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1)-0+(-1)(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =3(-2-2)+6 \\\\  & =-12+6 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-6|=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663395 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $VH$ of the $△\\,TUV,$ if $T(4,2,0),$ $U(1,3,2),$ $V(2,4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $VH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =u-t \\\\  & =(i+3j+2k)-(4i+2j) \\\\  & =-3i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =v-t \\\\  & =(2i+4j)-(4i+2j) \\\\  & =-2i+2j \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2)i-((-3)\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 2)j+((-3)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-4i-4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,-4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{2}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3} & =VH\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{42}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663403 :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,GHI$, if $G(1,1,1)$, $H(3,2,1)$, $I(2,2,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GI}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GH} & =h-g \\\\  & =(3i+2j+k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+0k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{GI} & =i-g \\\\  & =(2i+2j+2k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GH}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GI} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)j+(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{GH}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GHI$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663409 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(3,1,2),$ $B(6,5,6),$ $C(2,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(6i+5j+6k)-(3i+j+2k) \\\\  & =3i+4j+4k \\\\  & =⟨3,4,4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j+k)-(3i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 4)i-(3\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 4)j+(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =-8i-j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-1,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{114}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{114}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663411 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $EH$ of the $△\\,DEF$, if $D(2,1,2)$, $E(2,-3,4)$, $F(1,0,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $EH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DEF$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}EH\\cdot DF\n$$\n\nSo, to find $EH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =(2i-3j+4k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-4j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(i+3k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)i-(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(0\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-4))k \\\\  & =-2i-2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{2}=\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $DF$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DF & =|\\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}EH\\cdot DF,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}EH\\cdot DF \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}EH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =EH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ EH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ EH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663412 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(3,2,0),$ $B(4,4,1),$ $C(4,3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+4j+k)-(3i+2j) \\\\  & =i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+3j+2k)-(3i+2j) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)j+(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =3i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{66}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663434 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,1,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,1,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 6-1\\cdot 0)i-((-1)\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 0)j+((-1)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =6i+6j-5k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{97}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663436 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,LMN,$ if $L(5,4,2),$ $M(9,2,5),$ $N(1,5,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(9i+2j+5k)-(5i+4j+2k) \\\\  & =4i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(i+5j+k)-(5i+4j+2k) \\\\  & =-4i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 3)i-(4\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 3)j+(4\\cdot 1-(-4)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-i-8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-8,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186664095 :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff3a9b43deb13eb7ffdc3c8f1ac918c8ea1925c49418fc25038179e993de6093.png)\n\nThe total cost and revenue per month (in dollars) of the restaurant can be modeled by the equations $y=750+7x$ and $y=11x+86,$ respectively, where $x$ is the number of units sold. \n\nSolve the system below using the substitution method to find the exact number of units that need to be produced to break even and the revenue generated at this point.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=750+7x \\\\ y &=86+11x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Note**: A company breaks even when the total revenue equals the total cost and is represented by the intersection of the revenue and cost functions.\n\nThe restaurant breaks even when$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$units are produced. \n\nThe revenue generated when selling this number of units is$\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that $y$ is isolated in the first equation. Therefore, we can replace $y$ with $750+7x$ in the second equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =86+11x \\\\ 750+7x & =86+11x \\\\ 750+7x-7x & =86+11x-7x \\\\ 750 & =86+4x \\\\ 750-86 & =86+4x-86 \\\\ 664 & =4x \\\\ \\frac{664}{4} & =\\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ 166 & =x \\\\ x & =166\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=166$ into $y=86+11x$, then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=86+11(166)=1\\,912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $x=166$ and $y=1\\,912.$\nTherefore, the restaurant breaks even when $166$ units are produced, and the revenue generated when selling this number of units is $\\$1\\,912.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186666149 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a43e4291dcbe0819d5c320b21805c992f32789123622ab01e7d407a45540defc.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ and $-4\\mathbf{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $\\frac{3}{2}a$ and $-4a$ must be parallel to $a.$\n\nNow, for the vector $\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ of the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$ have the same direction since the factor $\\frac{3}{2}$ is positive.\n\n![](images/59/59aa29fdfd338165f1b6fa8cd3a4d2a8d30fae92023ab855b0df88e38a24c5cd.png)\n\n \n\nNext, for the vector $-4a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-4a$ is $4$ times the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $-4a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-4$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is the following.\n\n![](images/aa/aab2a72ca63e9142f502bbfbf2c5dd31d3a452860422d6088bf75ae060ef29b6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670002 :question/problem "![](images/53/53aa9f55c84bab63cc1bbfcc396d617287f8a2c7740c9749521289b22237a622.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta$ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-2,4).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the lower semicircle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle.\n\n![](images/e4/e43fa60cee504db4449c575aa0a01f7b157c1774db0f88939c1f98192716891e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$, we get the required lower semicircle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the lower semicircle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670014 :question/problem "![](images/6d/6de586fe8fe643862433f8597a6937fc05893c1ba5b141c358afdbaae7b72c70.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(3,7)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,7).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(3,7).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +7,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670016 :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6a2c51fec19e92910a4723e6ceeb63312ab1082f7aff5eee49c1ef55e56c83c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $7$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=7.$ Substituting $r=7$ gives\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670023 :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a619839e28cb0caaba42366b5a5a573b348e8b53a213c37319d7eb56dc5e859.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta$ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=8.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/8a/8ab3cf3e55a699da8fd5cd552d58a2f61ccd8acd96c2161e1d0a1bb7b79800ac.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670027 :question/problem "![](images/f7/f73702f7b4408148fe51e3361f32cfc791d0fc5dae45c39380f81d567d4fe397.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta$ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=5.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/9d/9de42c7154740e93f944661bdefc916044af581264ec2af0db9af624e7680777.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670039 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =25({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =25(1) \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670050 :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b64ef6f76519e424e27b14eeaa6d090fa3a0fc94ab8381e5a36a7fc2fbe0a74.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $1$ centered at $(2,1)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(2,1).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670051 :question/problem "![](images/61/61ebe29c5b9a45ea98b1137b0cde94adff7e5d43b3da4e89fc5cab938770db13.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/19/19288384020e643b0335f7aaaea8875839b5e33ba69594ac75a5ee1fd63301a0.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670080 :question/problem "![](images/d9/d98b8895d8b6322729c25533584e3ef686cfd068f05363f2afd9f207d539a70a.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $13$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(13,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=13.$ Substituting $r=13$ gives\n\n$$\nx=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670092 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674190 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674193 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((-8)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674204 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=25.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=25.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 5,25).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 25\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca54518b097929ecf8756bc699c5fef32de07b174134781e778fdcc395e964fe.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{25}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}[v]_{u^{2}}^{25}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}(25)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}(25 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 25 u]_{-5}^{5} \\\\ &=1(((125)+\\left(- \\frac{125}{3}\\right))-((-125)+\\left(\\frac{125}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{500}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{500}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674214 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=36.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=36.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=36\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 6,36).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-6\\le u\\le 6,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 36\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/13/132cec4e3586ef176e2c94e05bbe8860063cce2cef4d0835159da844ea43b8ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{36}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[v]_{u^{2}}^{36}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 36 u]_{-6}^{6} \\\\ &=1(((216)+(-72))-((-216)+(72))) \\\\ &=1(288) \\\\ &=288\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674221 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((-8)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674232 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-1-2x)^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{3},\\,g=-1-2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3g^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1-2x)^{2} \\\\  &=-6(-1-2x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674233 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-9+3x)^{2}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{2},\\,g=-9+3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=2g\\cdot (3) \\\\  &=6(-9+3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674235 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(8-5x)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=8-5x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (8-5x)^{3} \\\\  &=-20(8-5x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674243 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(5+3x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=5+3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (3) \\\\  &=12(5+3x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674246 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-7+6x)^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{5},\\,g=-7+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=5g^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=5\\cdot 6\\cdot (-7+6x)^{4} \\\\  &=30(-7+6x)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674306 :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-7x+16}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-7x+16.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot -7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x+16}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x+16}})|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7}{2\\sqrt{-7(0)+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7}{2\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674308 :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}$ at the point where $x=3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2{x}^{2}+6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x+6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+3)}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(\\frac{2x+3}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(3)+6}{2\\sqrt{2(3{)}^{2}+6(3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674309 :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}$ at the point where $x=4$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4{x}^{2}-7x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (8x-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={(\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}})|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(4)-7}{2\\sqrt{4(4{)}^{2}-7(4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674315 :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-5{x}^{2}+9x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9-10x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (9-10x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-10(1)}{2\\sqrt{-5(1{)}^{2}+9(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674322 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{37+7x}$ at the point where $x=-4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=37+7x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-4} & ={(\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7x}})|}_{x=-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7(-4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682442 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4{x}^{2}-4.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (8x) \\\\  & =\\frac{8x}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682445 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+12x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=7{x}^{2}+12x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=14x+12.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (14x+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(7x+6)}{2\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+12x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7x+6}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+12x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682448 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{-7x-4}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-7x-4.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot -7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682451 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{-5x-8}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-5x-8.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot -5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{-5x-8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
